Interstitial Cystitis / Bladder Pain Syndrome
Complex Fatigue Entities
Chronic neurogenic bladder inflammation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-022
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of intractable suprapubic pain, severe dysuria, and autonomic panic to rule out acute complicated pyelonephritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden, unremitting bladder agony with voiding required every 10 minutes, accompanied by severe distress and hyperventilation. Immediate assessment was required to definitively exclude acute ascending renal infection or urolithiasis, and to provide potent neuro-modulating analgesia to de-escalate the pain crisis before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My bladder feels like it is filled with battery acid and razor blades; I am in so much pain I cannot breathe properly.
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known history of Interstitial Cystitis presents with a severe acute exacerbation. The current flare evolved over 12 hours, starting with mild pressure and rapidly escalating to unrelenting suprapubic pain and severe urinary urgency/frequency. The patient reports passing only drops of urine with intense urethral burning. Central sensitization is prominent, with the patient experiencing severe lower abdominal allodynia and profound fatigue from continuous pain and lack of sleep.
Functional Status: Confined to the bathroom floor; unable to walk due to pelvic floor spasm.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme psychological distress and pain catastrophizing; high risk of accidental overdose from desperation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/95 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Curled in fetal position, crying, tachycardic and tachypneic secondary to extreme pain.
Physical Exam: Abdomen exhibits profound suprapubic tenderness and hyperesthesia extending to the bilateral lower quadrants. No costovertebral angle tenderness bilaterally. Bowel sounds are normal. External genitalia structurally normal; no purulent discharge. Presentation is archetypal for chronic neurogenic bladder inflammation and severe pelvic floor spasm. Urinalysis via dipstick reveals trace hematuria, negative nitrites, and negative leukocytes.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty OTC pain relief packets visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Maximum doses of Ural and Paracetamol consumed with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Interstitial Cystitis / Bladder Pain Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, lack of flank/costovertebral tenderness, and negative leukocyte/nitrite dipstick.

Urolithiasis (Renal Colic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is strictly suprapubic and related to bladder filling/voiding, rather than classical unilateral radiating loin-to-groin colic.

Acute Urinary Retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient continuously voiding small volumes; bladder is not distended on deep palpation once spasm allows.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of agonizing pelvic pain and severe dysuria closely mimicked an acute complicated urinary tract infection or obstructing calculus, requiring an immediate after-hours home visit to test urine and physically exclude an ascending renal infection. Having confirmed a sterile, neuro-inflammatory bladder crisis, urgent pharmacological intervention targeting nerve wind-up and muscle spasm was necessary, as standard analgesia was completely ineffective, leaving the patient in a severe state of physical and emotional collapse.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Diazepam 5mg once only to abruptly break the pelvic floor muscle spasm cycle. Prescribed Amitriptyline 25mg stat and then nocte for central neuro-modulation. Targeted intervention strategy includes Amitriptyline, PEA, and LDN.
Positive Behavioral Change: By utilizing a hot water bottle continuously on the suprapubic area, you will actively override the local nerve endings and relax the detrusor muscle, directly decreasing the sensation of urgency and rewarding yourself with longer intervals between voiding.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to specialist pelvic pain physiotherapist.
DME/Safety Optional: Heat packs recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to optimize chronic neuro-modulators.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to ED immediately if high fever develops, if severe pain shifts to the mid-back (flanks), or if gross hematuria (frank blood clots) appears.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of sterile cystitis requires an underlying negative urine culture; a formal MCS will be sent tomorrow.
Clinical Course: The acute hyperalgesic bladder flare will slowly down-regulate over 48-72 hours with muscle relaxants and neuropathic pain agents.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department if you develop a fever and shivering, or if you completely stop passing urine for more than 6 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bladder wall is not infected; rather, the nerves are inflamed and sending false "damage" signals to your brain. The medications specifically calm these nerves and relax the cramped pelvic muscles.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate all acidic foods, caffeine, and alcohol strictly for the next week; removing chemical irritants prevents further nerve stimulation in the bladder, reinforcing a faster return to baseline comfort.

